// modules/overview/guesses/data/cells.js -- PURE DATA, written by hand (not generated by tools/build-guess-data.mjs).
// Cross-tabulations of the Federal Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances 2025 that the published tables do not carry: the
// survey's own tables give a median by income group OR by age group, never both at once, so a guess built from the two
// overshoots at young ages and at both income extremes (a 20-year-old on $40,000 got $32,000 of investments; the survey's
// holders under 35 in the bottom 60% by income have a median of $2,900). Each cell below is the weighted median (or the
// stated percentile) among families that hold the item, from the public microdata (5 implicates, survey weights, age of the
// oldest adult, income as the survey's own income groups), computed 2026-11-07 for the guess-sanity check (qa/guess-sanity.mjs
// holds the same cells to test against; docs: modules/overview/guesses/README.md "Believable for the household").
// Refresh with each SCF (every three years; the next is the 2028 survey, released late 2029).
// Ids are registered in compute.js with the other tables, so "Where our guesses come from" lists them.
